70 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 22 0 R/E(Turn to Psalm 100 and read all five verses aloud together. It is a psalm that cannot sit still: it calls the whole earth to shout, to sing, to come through God's gates with thanksgiving. And in the middle of all that noise sits the quiet fact everything this year will stand on: 'It is He who has made us, and not we ourselves; we are His people and the sheep of His pasture' \(Psalm 100:3\). You did not make yourself. God did, on purpose, down to the last hidden cell. Hold that, because it opens a question worth chasing all year: if the God who so carefully built our bodies also planted the gardens, might He have tucked real help for those bodies inside the plants? This week's herb, the dark elderberry, is the first of thirty-six clues. For centuries families simmered its dark berries into a winter syrup, sensing that God had tucked something good inside. As you close the psalm, thank Him you are not an accident but His handiwork, and when your body fails you, be still and trust the One who made it.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

71 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 22 0 R/E(God designed elderberry's dark purple berries with compounds that help the body's own immune system fight off sickness.)/Pg 11 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

