219 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 25 0 R/E(Almost four hundred years ago, a doctor named Martin Blochwich wrote a book, in Latin, all about one shrub, the elder. In English it was called Anatomia Sambuci, or The Anatomy of the Elder. A man who signed himself C. de Iryngio turned it into English, and he dated his note 'At the Camp in Athol, June 30. 1651.' The book tells how to make a 'Rob.' Press the juice from ripe berries, strain it, and thicken it 'on a soft and clear fire.' Some people added 'a little sugar.' It says women gave it to their 'coughing unquiet children.' Later, the writer John Evelyn said that if the elder's uses were well known, a countryman could 'fetch a remedy from every hedge.' The God who planted the hedges tucked help inside them too.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[8]>>
endobj

220 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 25 0 R/E(In the wild, elder grows along the damp edges of European woodlands and hedgerows, where the soil stays cool and moist. That cool, wet home is part of why its berry is cooling and drying for the body. A hardy shrub, elder grows outdoors in Zones 5 to 8, returning each spring. Find your zone and see if an elder could grow where you live.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[9]>>
endobj

221 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 25 0 R/E(ART ACTIVITY)/Pg 14 0 R/K[10]>>
endobj

222 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 25 0 R/E(In your notebook's art box, draw a large, fancy capital E the way old books decorated the first letter of a page. Fill its edges with elder leaves, lacy white flowers, and clusters of blue-black berries, and add gold or bright color. Letter beneath it: 'A remedy from every hedge.')/Pg 14 0 R/K[11]>>
endobj

223 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 25 0 R/E(Go further \(grown-ups\): sculpt the letter as a relief tile in air-dry clay, press real elder leaves into the wet clay for texture, and brush it gold once it dries.)/Pg 14 0 R/K[12]>>
endobj

